1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,954
Slide 16 examines GPU ACCELERATION VS.

2
00:00:03,954 --> 00:00:10,700
CPU OVERLOAD. Sarah, what is the trade-off in desktop graphics rendering?

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:22,817
Look at the left card: When Hardware Acceleration is enabled, WebView2 offloads all the window blur effects, transparent rounded corners, and animations to the GPU.

4
00:00:22,817 --> 00:00:26,890
CPU usage stays at 1%, and animations are buttery smooth!

5
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:38,550
BUT on thin-and-light ultrabooks with integrated Intel Iris graphics, running both a 4K external monitor and GPU blur shaders causes the chip to heat up to 85°C and thermal throttle!

6
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:48,286
Look at the right card: If you disable GPU acceleration, the CPU has to calculate every single transparent pixel in software.

7
00:00:48,286 --> 00:00:56,130
CPU usage spikes to 30%, but it guarantees stability on virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI) like Citrix!

8
00:00:56,530 --> 00:01:05,670
As enterprise architects, you must know when to enable GPU offload for local workstations and when to force software rasterization for enterprise VDI fleets!

9
00:01:06,070 --> 00:01:10,220
One size does NOT fit all in enterprise desktop engineering!

10
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:16,980
True engineering mastery lies in understanding the trade-offs across diverse hardware environments.

